 okay  i can pass it on  yeah you've landed on your feet i'm also  a big fan of jane's writing i was very  disappointed um but jumping into bruised  what was it about this character in this  script that said oh i want to do this  well when i read it um i  i found bobby buddha khan to be quite  mysterious there's not lots of  information about her in the script  which is always really intriguing  because  you know that can be one of two things  either the parts underwritten  or the parts leaving you lots of face to  um  you know fill in the blanks and and and  you know figure out for yourself what  that mystery is about and then make a  decision about whether how how clear  that is to the audience or not you know  how much you wear that on the surface or  not  um so i felt like there was lots of  scope with this character and i was also  just really excited to be in a film  about um female fighters you know i was  like oh i haven't really seen something  like this before  um you know so to be a part of that  world was really cool and then the added  factor of being able to be british in it  which was something that kind of came by  accident but really made sense once we  decided on it was also a nice thing to  be able to do  uh halle berry uh  it's it this is very ambitious for her  directorial debut she's starring it's an  mma role it's i mean it's just like  she bit off so much and think i mean it  uh what was it like watching her uh do  this because this is like she really  went for it with this you know  everything about this  oh yeah it was it was amazing and you  know people say the term life changing  often and they kind of throw it around  in a way that i think is a bit flippant  but i really mean it you know to watch  somebody like halle berry  who  for my whole life has felt kind of you  know inaccessible in the sense that  that's that's halle berry she's got this  kind of huge  label around her and she's the only  black female  uh to win oscar for best actress so far  and you know she's it was a part of my  childhood when i was watching film  growing up  um  and i was watching her  both in her element and in new territory  at the same time you know a very  experienced actress but also somebody  who is embarking upon a new project as a  director and then doing those two things  simultaneously and then doing her own  stunts you know and  she worked so hard um it was  it was incredible to see her tenacity to  see her work ethic and to see the the  grace with which she handled it was just  it was so wonderful so i really took a  lot from that as somebody who wants to  try and do  you know not just acting but other  different things as well i took so much  from that experience  i love learning about the behind the  scenes of the making of movies and tv  what do you think might surprise people  to learn about the making of bruised or  um you know if you have any really cool  behind-the-scenes stories you'd like to  share  i think what was what was so lovely  about not so much surprising but so  lovely about the project was that hallie  really wanted to feature new jersey you  know and the actual community of new  jersey and really get an essence of  the spirit of the place into the film so  that it wasn't just a kind of ambiguous  location where we were filming because  it looked right for the for the picture  um  and i remember  one day you know our trailers were at  one point on the side of the road and  hallie was walking out to go to set and  there was a lady driving a school bus  and she stopped and she let out the  window and she was like oh my god is  that the halle berry movie  and harley turned around and was like  yeah was like oh my god and all these  kids were in the back like didn't know  what was going on um and i found that  really kind of endearing you know seeing  this sort of  you know hollywood and the local  community kind of crashing together in a  really sort of beautiful way so i really  enjoyed that